{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_jtfvrfr5xwb3qqzwyzvx73t4lm6n7qpgixwinsxa4zvhloupnjpa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete. All four phases done, exit code 0 across the board.\n\n## What the library is\n\nA single 28-line C function, `void gaussian_kernel(float *dest, int size, float radius)` — one exported symbol, one translation unit, **no** `[features]` in `Cargo.toml` and **no** `#ifdef`/options in `CMakeLists.txt`, so there is exactly **one** valid build configuration (verified mechanically, not assumed).\n\n## Completion gate\n\n- **`SYMBOLS.md`** — C `.so` exports exactly `gaussian_kernel`; the Rust `.so` exports it under the identical name. `nm -D` diff is empty; 0 unresolved non-libc symbols. No C source was left untranslated.\n- **Phase B** — all 20 `CONFIGS.md` rows pass with randomized inputs (fixed seed): size parity/sign/boundaries (`i32::MIN` … 65537), radius classes (±0, ±inf, quiet+signalling NaN, subnormals, powers of two, `MAX`/`MIN`, random bit patterns), buffer offsets, unaligned pointers, repeated calls.\n- **Phase C** — all 20 `ERRORS.md` rows have passing error-path tests. Since the C API is `void` with no error channel, every row is a degenerate-input path derived by grepping the C: the negative/NaN clamp, the `sum > 0` guard, the `size <= -2` no-op, `NULL` with a non-writing size, and the deliberate one-element buffer overrun for even `size`. Two rows (NULL with `size >= -1`, `size == i32::MAX`) are unbounded UB in both and are documented with the closest survivable assertion instead.\n- **Feature combos** — the single combination, run in both debug and release.\n\n## Two real issues found and fixed\n\n1. **Rust aborted where C worked.** Debug builds insert a misaligned-pointer check, so an unaligned `dest` (which C handles fine via `movss`) aborted the process. Fixed in `src/lib.rs` with `write_unaligned`/`read_unaligned` — bit-identical for aligned input, now matching C for unaligned input in every profile. This is the only change to the translation.\n\n2. **The harness was silently testing a stale artifact.** `cargo test` does *not* rebuild a `cdylib` (integration tests never link it). My first mutation run proved this: six deliberately broken versions of `src/lib.rs` **all passed**. After adding a mandatory `cargo build` plus an `assert_not_stale` guard in the test harness, 5 of 6 mutations were caught (19–36 failing tests each); the sixth (`s2` rounded through `f64`) is genuinely equivalent for that one constant.\n\nI also re-ran everything against `-O2`/`MinSizeRel` C builds via a `HARVEST_C_SO` override — at `-O2` GCC constant-folds `expf(5.76)` with MPFR, and the Rust result still matches bit-for-bit. `c_src/` was not modified (optimized builds went to `$TMPDIR`).\n\nOne caveat worth flagging: had I trusted the initial \"40 passed\" result, I would have reported success on an unverified translation. The staleness guard is what makes the green result meaningful; re-run via `./run_verification.sh` rather than bare `cargo test`."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4383,"cache_read_input_tokens":104164,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4383,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"78ad3e7d-e8ec-4d1d-962b-cc334325ed55","uuid":"1abd23f7-3d9b-4634-b2bd-99cf66cef4e1","timestamp":"2026-08-19T12:34:22.818Z"}
